---
name: open-sora-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices and guidelines for Open Sora applications
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Utilize the built-in state management for efficient data handling.
- Leverage the component library for consistent UI elements.
- Implement lazy loading for components to enhance performance.
- Use hooks for managing side effects and component lifecycle effectively.

---
name: react-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices for React applications
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Use functional components and hooks for cleaner code.
- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility.
- Use PropTypes or TypeScript for type checking.
- Optimize performance with React.memo and useCallback.

---
name: redux-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices for Redux state management
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Use Redux Toolkit for simplified store setup and reducers.
- Normalize state shape to avoid deeply nested structures.
- Use selectors for accessing state to improve performance.
- Implement middleware for handling side effects (e.g., redux-thunk).

---
name: jest-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices for testing with Jest
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Write unit tests for all components and utility functions.
- Use snapshot testing for React components to catch UI changes.
- Mock external dependencies to isolate tests.
- Organize tests in a dedicated folder structure for clarity.

---
name: eslint-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices for ESLint configuration and usage
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Use a shared ESLint configuration for consistency across the team.
- Enable TypeScript support with appropriate plugins.
- Set up rules for code style and best practices (e.g., no unused variables).
- Regularly run ESLint as part of the development workflow.

---
name: testing-library-best-practices.mdc
description: Best practices for testing with React Testing Library
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx}
---

- Focus on testing user interactions and behavior rather than implementation details.
- Use queries that resemble how users find elements (e.g., getByRole).
- Avoid using waitFor unless necessary; prefer synchronous queries.
- Keep tests isolated and avoid dependencies on external state.

Feel free to adjust the globs and practices based on your project's specific needs and structure.